{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FJ9EEdfdtbtg8quvqZtx5v","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough walk of the Almanac codebase, here is the architectural friction inventory:\n\n---\n\n## Architectural Friction Inventory\n\n### Shallow Modules\n\n**Role config wrappers.** `almanac_converge_role_field()` (converge-core.sh:56–61) and `almanac_harden_role_field()` (harden-core.sh:61–69) are ~8-line passthroughs over `almanac_loop_role_config()`. They pipe the raw output through awk to extract one TSV field—the same pattern. The abstraction cost (function call + awk invocation) nearly equals the direct call. **Candidates for inlining or unification into a shared helper.**\n\n**Provider dispatch bottleneck.** `almanac_provider_call()` (agent.sh:74–82) wraps function lookup + dispatch in a 9-line function that does `printf -v` a function name and calls it. Direct function invocation would be equally readable and require no wrapper. The indirection is architectural (decoupling callers from provider names) but adds a call-stack frame to every provider operation.\n\n### Cross-Module Duplication\n\n**Plan/rubric directory patterns.** converge-core.sh defines `almanac_converge_plan_dir()` and harden-core.sh defines `almanac_harden_rubric_path()`—both follow the pattern `printf '%s/docs/plans/<type>/%s\\n' \"$root\" \"$slug\"`. This is duplicated, not factored into a shared `almanac_loop_plan_base_dir()`.\n\n**Goal/target summaries.** `almanac_converge_goal_summary()` (converge-core.sh:356–358) truncates a string to 80 chars; harden has no equivalent but likely needs similar logic for dashboard rendering. Duplicated-by-omission.\n\n**Status field reader (TSV):** Both run.sh and worker.sh implement `almanac_loop_record_get()` / `almanac_loop_tsv_record_set()` using identical awk/while-read patterns. run.sh owns it; worker.sh delegates to `tsv_record_set()` for its schema, so the duplication is minimal but run.sh's generic engine is underutilized.\n\n**Overseer parsing.** `almanac_converge_overseer_parse()` (converge-core.sh:223–324) is 102 lines of state-machine parsing to extract `VERDICT|REASON|STEER|GOAL_UPDATE`. This is converge-specific but uses generic TSV-field patterns (prefix-match, leading-space trim, multi-line continuation). **Long function that should be promoted to lib/ if converge is expected to evolve.**\n\n### Long Functions in Core Modules\n\n**`almanac_converge_overseer_parse()`** — 102 lines. A state machine that reconstructs multi-line values from a chatty LLM output. Complex enough to deserve unit tests and a separate file (lib/converge-parse.sh or lib/parse.sh).\n\n**`almanac_converge_run()`** (converge-core.sh:850–986) — 137 lines. The main loop driver: registers the run, scaffolds the plan dir, runs worker/overseer rounds, applies goal updates, writes convergence. Does too much: orchestration + state-machine + file I/O + git-aware dirty-path tracking. **Should decompose into _run_worker_phase() and _run_overseer_phase() sub-functions.** The dirty-path tracking (`almanac_converge_dirty_paths()` + `almanac_converge_commit_agent_paths()`) is a mini-module (30+ lines) buried inside.\n\n**`almanac_harden_reviewer_prompt()`** (harden-core.sh:331–381) — 50 lines of heredoc-heavy prompt construction. Not complex but large and templated; typical of multi-line string assembly.\n\n### Loop-Adapter Inconsistency\n\n**Flag parsing style.** ralph.sh (lines 52–66) uses a case statement with `shift` + manual value extraction (`provider=\"$(printf '%s' \"${1:-}\" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')\"`). harden.sh (lines 48–61) and converge.sh (lines 60–76) use identical patterns. **Duplication of the flag-parse boilerplate across all three adapters.** A shared `_almanac_launch_parse_flags()` helper with a declarative spec (e.g., `--provider lowercased required`) could reduce this.\n\n**Positional vs. flag-based runner invocation.** ralph's `exec_argv` takes `(mode, prd, iterations)` as positional args; harden takes `(target, rounds)` positional; converge takes `(goal, mode, action, rounds, …)` with multiple optional params. **ralph and converge enforce their action mode (once/afk vs. prompt/exec) at launch; harden has no equivalent (lenses vs. mode).** The adapter interface is underspecified.\n\n**new_run_argv format.** ralph returns `ralph --prd X --mode Y …` (flags); harden returns `harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]` (mixed positional + flags). **Inconsistent shapes mean the hub's resume/clone logic may struggle.** The adapter contract should enforce a consistent shape (all flags or all positional).\n\n### Provider-Adapter Inconsistency\n\n**Result capture mechanism.** claude.sh implements `almanac_provider_claude_extract()` to recover the final result from the captured event stream (no --output-last-message flag). codex.sh has no extract hook (codex writes result via --output-last-message in argv). **The asymmetry is documented but means callers must branch on provider in a few places.** A unified result-file contract (always populate it, regardless of how) would simplify downstream code.\n\n**Sandbox handling.** claude.sh maps sandbox values to --permission-mode (read-only→plan, danger-full-access→bypassPermissions). codex.sh defaults sandbox to \"danger-full-access\" and passes it through verbatim to --sandbox. **codex's default is hardcoded; claude's requires explicit handling at every call site.** The seam should enforce one default.\n\n**Effort naming.** Both providers accept \"low/medium/high/xhigh\"; claude also accepts \"max\", codex accepts none. **The menus diverge; loop adapters that build effort arguments blind will send \"max\" to codex and fail.** The shared effort menu should only list common values.\n\n### Test Coverage Gaps\n\n**Converge overseer parsing.** `test-converge.sh` (51 KB) exists but the complex overseer-parse state machine (`almanac_converge_overseer_parse()`, 102 lines) likely has sparse unit tests. No dedicated test-parse.sh. **The state machine handles multi-line paragraphs and malformed LLM output — edge cases worth explicit test matrices.**\n\n**Harden ledger/findings.** test-harden-cli.sh (93 KB) tests the CLI but not the finding-ledger state machine (harden-core.sh:486–570). **Test coverage for `almanac_harden_ledger_append_entry()` and ledger-state consistency is unclear.**\n\n**Provider adapter contracts.** test-providers.sh (9 KB) exists but is minimal. **No tests verify claude vs. codex divergences (sandbox mapping, effort menus, result extraction).** A test matrix (`for provider in claude codex; do test_$feature; done`) would expose inconsistencies.\n\n**Loop-adapter new_run_argv/new_run_env round-trip.** Each adapter implements these but no unified test validates ralph's resume composes the same argv/env it decomposed. **The hub's resume logic depends on this invertibility; no test currently verifies it.**\n\n**Dirty-path tracking in converge.** `almanac_converge_dirty_paths()` and `almanac_converge_commit_agent_paths()` (lines 655–708) are critical to correctness (the bug that was observed in commit 4526a58 and fixed). **No dedicated test for the set-difference algorithm that filters pre-existing dirty paths.**"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b01beec-8e7f-499f-84c4-979f364c4a47","uuid":"8e0ade8c-fac9-436b-b782-bb7cea5e25fd","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:23:32.573Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Walk the Almanac codebase looking for architectural friction — places where modules are SHALLOW (interface nearly as complex as implementation), DUPLICATED across files, or LEAK across their seams. The domain vocabulary is in CLAUDE.md at the repo root and CONTEXT.md (already loaded into my context).\n\nKey modules to scrutinize:\n- lib/converge-core.sh (986 lines)\n- lib/harden-core.sh (1979 lines)\n- lib/run.sh (953 lines)\n- lib/loops/*.sh (3 loop adapters: ralph 213, harden 171, converge 272)\n- lib/providers/*.