{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011yuEKmK5v5u3Gn4rvzbQHk","type":"tool_result","content":"50\t| `skills/loop/harden-loop/` | User-facing harden-loop skill. |\n51\t| `skills/loop/converge-loop/` | User-facing converge-loop skill. |\n52\t\n53\t**The almanac launcher (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`).** Interactive run *configuration* is unified in one loop-agnostic launcher, `almanac_loop_launch <loop> [native flags…]`. The launcher itself does **not** branch on loop type — it dispatches the adapter's `launch` verb through the loop-adapter seam, so each loop's flag parsing, prompts, env-export, and runner exec live in its adapter file (`lib/loops/<name>.sh::almanac_loop_<name>_launch`). Adding a 4th loop is a single new file in `lib/loops/`; no central case-statement to keep in sync. What the launcher does still own is what every adapter uses identically: the field collectors (`_almanac_launch_need_provider`/`_choice`/`_positive_int` — pick a provider, resolve a model/effort, validate-and-re-prompt for numeric fields so a stray keystroke is a retry, not a crash) and the summary panel (`almanac_loop_launch_summary` — gum-styled or plain), all routed through the shared `almanac_loop_ui_*` gum-or-plain seam. Each adapter's `launch` verb summarises, confirms, and execs the runner — it never reimplements the run, only its config. The runner exec routes through the same adapter's `exec_argv` verb (`almanac_loop_adapter_call <type> exec_argv …` → `_ALMANAC_LOOP_ARGV`, see the loop-adapter seam below), so no central code hard-codes a loop's runner path: ralph's `…/ralph-loop/scripts/once.sh`/`afk.sh`, harden's `almanac harden <target> --loop`, and converge's `almanac converge --goal …` all live in their adapters. `cmd/ralph.sh`, the ralph skill `ralph.sh` (a thin delegate, so direct `bash ralph.sh` and `almanac ralph` share it), and the hub's New-run flow all route through `almanac_loop_launch`, so the config UX is identical everywhere and has a single source of truth.\n54\t\n55\t`cmd/doctor.sh` reports almanac's **optional-dependency** status so users know what is and isn't styled/available: `gum` (Charm — styles the harden/ralph dashboards + HITL prompts), `gh` (GitHub issue queues + CI watch), and `jq` (findings parsing). The gum check is the binary-presence helper `almanac_gum_present` (in `lib/core.sh`), distinct from the runtime, TTY-gated `almanac_loop_ui_has_gum` — `doctor` and the installers want \"is the binary installed\", not \"should I style this stream right now\". `almanac_report_gum` prints the verdict with an install hint, and both the claude-code and codex installers call it at the end of `almanac install`, so install **and** doctor surface gum's presence/absence. Every optional dep is exactly that — absent, the CLI degrades to plain output, keeping almanac's near-zero-dependency promise.\n56\t\n57\t`cmd/harden.sh` is the first harden-loop CLI surface. It uses `lib/harden-core.sh` to draft a target-specific `rubric.md` under `docs/plans/harden/<target-slug>/` in the caller repo, refusing to overwrite an existing contract. Drafts start with `Status: draft`; after the developer edits the contract, `almanac harden <target> --approve` marks it `Status: approved` and appends an approval timestamp. The approval is a **lock the loop enforces**: once a rubric has been drafted, `almanac_harden_rubric_approved` gates the fan-out, so a bare `almanac harden <target>` refuses (via `_die`) to run reviewers against an unapproved contract — the goalposts can only be set by a deliberate human approve. An explicit ad-hoc run with no rubric at all bypasses the gate and proceeds. The rubric is the **bar**: `almanac_harden_rubric_acceptance` reads the `## Acceptance` criteria back, and `almanac_harden_reviewer_prompt` embeds them so reviewers judge against the contract rather than an implicit standard. The bar grows only by ratified defects — `almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion` appends a new `- [ ]` criterion under `## Acceptance` append-only (monotonic, visible as a diff) and idempotent (an identical criterion is never re-added). Run with no flags, `almanac harden <target>` fans out one read-only reviewer per configured lens (`almanac_harden_fanout`), passing each reviewer the rubric path so the contract is consumed when present. The lens set comes from `almanac_harden_lenses` — the five PRD lenses (`correctness security perf edge-cases contracts`) by default, overridable at runtime via `HARDEN_LENSES` (comma- or whitespace-separated, no enforced cap). Each lens spawns a background worker through the shared worker orchestration with a `read-only` sandbox, resolving its provider/model/effort via `almanac_harden_role` (see **Per-role provider config** below) so lenses can mix providers. Reviewers run concurrently and never write to the target; once all finish, the parent aggregates each reviewer's findings into the ledger **sequentially** (so there are no concurrent ledger writers), deduped via `almanac_harden_ledger_record`, and prints each lens's findings (jq parse with an awk fallback). Worker pids are captured via a file rather than command substitution so the backgrounded workers stay children of the launching shell and remain waitable; a failed reviewer is reported and skipped without sinking the round. A missing target fails via `_die` before anything spawns.\n58\t\n59\tThe rubric is **immutable to agents during a run** — the goalposts move only by a deliberate human act (editing/approving the file between runs) or the conductor's controlled append at ratification (`almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion`, which runs outside the reviewer/fixer phase). The fan-out brackets the reviewer round with an integrity guard: `almanac_harden_rubric_snapshot` copies the rubric before any reviewer spawns and `almanac_harden_rubric_verify` checks it byte-for-byte once they finish, reverting and warning if it changed. Reviewers run read-only and cannot write the rubric, so this is defense-in-depth today; it becomes load-bearing for the write-capable fixer slice, which brackets its own agent call the same way (snapshot after ratification's legitimate appends, revert any fixer edit). An ad-hoc bare run with no drafted rubric has nothing to guard, so the snapshot returns cleanly and the guard is skipped.\n60\t\n61\tThe **findings ledger** persists adjudicated findings append-only to `findings.md` beside the rubric. The canonical finding schema is `lens, severity, location, claim, demonstration`; the ledger adds `id`, `status` (`open` | `fixed` | `rejected-subjective` | `wontfix-per-context`), `round`, and an `adjudication` note. `almanac_harden_parse_findings` normalizes a reviewer's JSON-Lines result into ledger-entry rows (new findings are `open`), dropping malformed lines cleanly. `id` is a `cksum` fingerprint of `(lens, location, claim)` — demonstration is excluded so a reworded repro is not a new finding — and `almanac_harden_ledger_record` dedupes against it, so a finding already adjudicated in a prior round is never re-added. `almanac_harden_ledger_open_blocking` reads the ledger back and returns only `status: open` findings, since fixed and rejected findings never gate the loop.\n62\t\n63\tThe **ratification engine** decides blocking vs. note by *executing* a finding's demonstration, never by trusting a reviewer's assertion. `almanac_harden_ratify` runs the demonstration through the execution seam `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces` — the **real ratify-by-execution executor** (PRD story 8): it builds a conductor prompt (`almanac_harden_ratify_prompt`) that tells the conductor to carry out the demonstration against the current code and end with a single machine token, runs it through the resolved conductor `(provider, model, effort)` via the shared agent runner (`workspace-write`, like the fixer, since judging by execution means actually running the proposed test/input), and reads the verdict back with `almanac_harden_ratify_verdict` (the affirmative literal `HARDEN_VERDICT=reproduces`; the negative cannot match it). It is conservative on every uncertainty — an empty demonstration, an unresolved provider, a crashed/un-runnable conductor call, or an un-parseable verdict all read as non-reproducing (a note) so opinions never silently gate the loop and a flaky provider never blocks a run. Tests drive the decision paths behind a fake provider (no real model calls). `almanac_harden_ratify` records the outcome in the ledger, printing one verdict: a new/open finding that reproduces → `blocking` (`status: open`); one that does not → `note` (`status: rejected-subjective`); an already-adjudicated finding (`fixed` / `rejected-subjective` / `wontfix-per-context`) that *newly* reproduces → `reopened` (back to `status: open`); one that still does not reproduce → `dropped` (left untouched, never re-litigated). Status transitions go through `almanac_harden_ledger_set_status` (rewrites status + adjudication in place); `almanac_harden_ledger_status` reads a finding's current status so new findings are told from already-adjudicated ones. When `almanac_harden_ratify` is given a rubric path, a finding that reproduces (`blocking` or `reopened`) also appends a falsifiable criterion to the rubric's `## Acceptance` via `almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion`, so the bar grows monotonically with every ratified defect; notes and drops never touch the rubric, so opinions cannot raise it.\n64\t\n65\tThe **single sequential fixer** (`almanac harden <target> --fix` → `almanac_harden_fix`) applies the open blocking findings. v1 is deliberately one write-capable agent — `sandbox=workspace-write` (never read-only), run in place with **no worktree** — so there are never concurrent writers to reconcile and any regression test it generates simply persists in the working tree (PRD: \"the deliverable is hardened code *plus the regression suite the loop built to prove it*\"). It reads the kill-list from `almanac_harden_ledger_open_blocking`, renders it into `almanac_harden_fixer_prompt` (which also embeds the rubric bar and tells the fixer to leave a failing-then-passing regression test per finding), and runs the fixer through the shared agent runner. The fixer's agent call is bracketed by the same rubric immutability guard the fan-out uses (`almanac_harden_rubric_snapshot` before, `almanac_harden_rubric_verify` after) — load-bearing here because the fixer *can* write the rubric; legitimate rubric growth happens earlier in ratification, outside this bracket. On a clean fixer exit every open blocking finding is marked `fixed` in the ledger (a still-broken one is reopened next round by reviewers/ratify, so nothing is silently closed); a fixer failure leaves the findings open and returns the provider's exit code. With no open blocking findings the fixer is a no-op (spawns no agent). The objective feedback gate is **decoupled** from the fixer so it can run once per round even when there is nothing to fix: `almanac_harden_report_feedback` runs the shared runner and prints a pass/fail verdict per loop. The convergence loop calls it as the round's last step (and reads its aggregate as the round's exit code); the standalone `--fix` CLI path runs it right after the fixer so its output is unchanged.\n66\t\n67\tThe **convergence loop** (`almanac harden <target> --loop` → `almanac_harden_run`) repeats hardening rounds until the target is bulletproof or the round budget is hit. One round (`almanac_harden_round`) runs **fan-out → ratify → fix → feedback** in sequence, threading the round number; its exit code is the feedback verdict (0 = all loops green). `almanac_harden_ratify_open` adjudicates every open finding the fan-out recorded by executing its demonstration through the seam (reproducing → stays blocking and grows the rubric bar; otherwise → non-blocking note); that rubric append is the conductor's sanctioned growth and runs *between* fan-out and fix, outside the immutability brackets those phases use. After each round `almanac_harden_run` prints the **kill-list** (the blocking findings still open) and a **verdict**. The verdict comes from the **convergence gate** `almanac_harden_gate_verdict` — a pure predicate over `(acceptance_met, open_blocking, round, budget)` with no I/O, unit-tested across its whole input space: it returns `converged` (exit 0) when acceptance is met **and** zero open blocking findings remain, `non-converged` (exit 2) when the budget is reached without convergence, and `continue` (exit 1) otherwise. Convergence is checked first, so a round that converges exactly at the budget is never mislabelled, and the gate never returns `continue` once the budget is reached, so the loop always terminates. Acceptance for the gate combines the round's feedback verdict with `almanac_harden_acceptance_met` (no unchecked `- [ ]` criteria left in the rubric's `## Acceptance`; vacuously met for an ad-hoc run with no rubric). The budget is configurable via `--rounds N` (default 5, or `HARDEN_ROUND_BUDGET`). On a `continue` verdict the loop pauses at a **HITL checkpoint** (`almanac_harden_hitl_checkpoint`) offering three choices: `HARDEN_HITL` (`ship`|`continue`|`steer`) drives it non-interactively, a TTY prompts via `gum choose`/`gum input` when present and a plain `read` otherwise, and an unattended run defaults to continue. **Ship** stops the loop and accepts the current state; **continue** runs another round; **steer** captures a directive (from `HARDEN_STEER` or the prompt, echoed on the checkpoint's stdout with the return code `2`) that `almanac_harden_run` carries forward and threads — via `almanac_harden_round` → `almanac_harden_fanout`/`almanac_harden_fix` → the reviewer and fixer prompts — into every subsequent round, so the operator can redirect or amend the run between rounds without babysitting each worker (PRD story 18). The directive persists until steered again; a rubric amendment is also picked up between rounds, since the fan-out re-reads and re-snapshots `rubric.md` each round.\n68\t\n69\t`cmd/converge.sh` is the generic loop sibling to ralph and harden. It launches with `almanac converge --goal <goal> --exec <cmd> [--rounds N] [--no-oversee] [--oversee-every N]`, stores canonical state under `docs/plans/converge/<slug>/`, registers `type=converge` in the shared run registry, and can later render status, watch, or stop via `almanac converge <slug> [--watch|--stop]`. `lib/converge-core.sh` owns the whole converge lifecycle: slug/scaffold, worker prompt and editable `prompt.md`, read-only overseer prompt, conservative four-field verdict parse, one-shot `.converge-steer`, `.converge-stop`, direct `goal.md` mutation with `goal.history.log`, final `convergence.md`, dashboard frame, and EXIT-trap-safe run finalization. Unlike ralph's PRD queue or harden's lens fan-out, converge runs exactly one user-supplied command per round; convergence is an overseer judgment against the current mutable goal. The worker and overseer both use `lib/agent.sh`, `lib/role.sh`, and `lib/run.sh` directly, so converge is a first-class shared-engine consumer rather than a fork.\n70\t\n71\t**Per-role provider config.** Every harden role selects its own `(provider, model, effort)` through `almanac_harden_role <role> [lens]` (with `almanac_harden_role_field` returning a single field), the one place harden's per-role defaults live. The three roles are **conductor** (judges/ratifies), **reviewer** (one per lens), and **fixer** (the single sequential writer). It layers over the shared loop resolver, most specific first: `HARDEN_<ROLE>[_<LENS>]_{PROVIDER,MODEL,EFFORT}` → `HARDEN_<ROLE>_*` → `HARDEN_*` → harden's default (provider `claude`; the provider's own default model/effort). Only reviewers consult the lens, so providers can be **mixed across lenses** in one round; conductor and fixer ignore it. Resolution reads only `HARDEN_*` env — never any host marker — so the tuple is identical whether the run was launched from Claude Code or Codex. The fan-out resolves each reviewer per lens and the fixer resolves its own; the convergence loop announces the resolved conductor at start and hands its identity to the execution seam (`almanac_harden_demo_reproduces`, via `almanac_harden_ratify`) for every ratified finding, so the provider that *judges* findings — by actually executing each demonstration through the agent runner — is the configured conductor, mixable per role and independent of the launching host.\n72\t\n73\tConverge roles use the same resolver with a `CONVERGE_` prefix: `CONVERGE_AGENT_{PROVIDER,MODEL,EFFORT}` controls the write-capable worker, `CONVERGE_OVERSEER_{PROVIDER,MODEL,EFFORT}` controls the read-only overseer, and `CONVERGE_{PROVIDER,MODEL,EFFORT}` is the consumer-wide fallback. Adding another loop consumer should mirror this pattern: define role names, document the env prefix, route calls through `almanac_loop_role_field`/`almanac_loop_role_config`, and keep provider defaults out of command parsing.\n74\t\n75\t**Supervision dashboard.** A run is supervised through a `gum`-styled redraw dashboard composed from live run state: reviewer status (per lens, with health), the round, findings tallies, rubric progress, and the feedback verdict. Following the PRD's \"render logic is pure\" rule, the composer `almanac_harden_dashboard_rows` is a **pure function** — it reads worker rows from stdin as `id<TAB>provider<TAB>health` TSV and takes the round/budget, findings tally, rubric progress, and feedback verdict as args, returning the printable rows with no file I/O, clock, or terminal, so it is unit-tested across all five fields without a terminal. The gather helpers feed it real state: `almanac_harden_dashboard_worker_rows` reads each worker's `status.tsv` and classifies its health via the shared detector; `almanac_harden_findings_tally` counts the ledger by status (`open=N fixed=N notes=N`, where notes = `rejected-subjective` + `wontfix-per-context`); `almanac_harden_rubric_progress` reports `checked/total` over the `## Acceptance` checklist; and `almanac_harden_latest_run_id` locates the target's most recent run by directory mtime (a glob+mtime scan, not `ls -t | head`, which would trip SIGPIPE under `set -o pipefail`). `almanac_harden_render_dashboard` ties these together and wraps the composed rows in `almanac_loop_ui_render`, which styles them as a rounded `gum` panel on a TTY and **degrades to plain passthrough** when `gum` is absent (or `ALMANAC_NO_GUM=1`, or output is piped) — keeping almanac's near-zero-dependency promise. It is safe to call before any run/worker/rubric state exists: it degrades to an empty reviewer list and zeroed tallies rather than failing.\n76\t\n77\tThe dashboard is **watched live** through `almanac_harden_dashboard_redraw` (the `almanac harden <target> --watch` mode and the hub's \"watch a run\" action): a redraw loop that, each frame, clears the terminal (TTY only, via the shared `almanac_loop_ui_clear` — never polluting piped output) and reprints `almanac_harden_render_dashboard` from the run's live worker/ledger/rubric state, so reviewer health, findings tallies, rubric progress, and the verdict update as the run progresses. Termination is bounded by construction so it can never run forever: a positive frame cap renders exactly that many frames (the unit-test path); off a TTY an uncapped call renders a single frame and returns (so a pipe/script never blocks); on a TTY it redraws until the latest run's workers have all finished (`almanac_harden_run_has_active_worker`, which keys on each round's per-worker `status.tsv` for liveness — the loop-level run-status blob tracks round/verdict) or there is no run to watch. A single worker's live event stream is watched through `almanac_harden_watch_worker` (`--watch-worker <lens|worker-id>`), which resolves the target's most recent run, accepts a bare lens as shorthand for its `reviewer-<lens>` worker, and delegates to the shared `almanac_loop_worker_watch` — tailing the worker's `events.jsonl` live (`tail -f`) on a TTY and printing its current contents once otherwise. The **steer flow** completes the supervision half: the HITL checkpoint's `steer` choice (above) lets the operator redirect or amend a run between rounds, threading a directive into the subsequent rounds' agents.\n78\t\n79\t**Interactive hub.** Bare `almanac` in a TTY (and `almanac hub` always) opens the **hub** (`cmd/hub.sh`) — the front door to every loop, built as a thin reader over the run registry rather than a retrofit. Its read views are **pure registry projections** so they are testable off a terminal and degrade to plain text without gum. `almanac_loop_hub_render <root> <which> [limit]` projects the registry into one of two lists: `running` emits every run whose lifecycle status is still `running`, carrying its live `round`/`summary` from the run-status blob, and surfaces a running entry whose pid is gone as `stale` (via `almanac_loop_run_is_stale`) so a crashed run is visible rather than silently lost; `recent` emits the finished runs (done/failed/aborted) newest-finish-first, capped at `limit` (default 10). `almanac_loop_hub_overview` composes both sections (with empty-state lines) and frames them through the shared `almanac_loop_ui_render`, so the hub shows a rounded gum panel on a TTY and plain text without gum. Off a TTY `cmd/hub.sh` renders that overview once and exits — it never blocks on a menu, so `almanac hub | cat`, scripts, and tests get a clean read view (`tests/test-hub.sh` drives the real `bin/almanac` chain: bare-almanac-off-a-TTY prints help and does *not* open the hub; `almanac hub` renders the running + recent overview from a seeded registry). The **per-run actions** layer on this read view: selecting a running loop offers **watch**, **stop**, and **queue-steer**, each a pure-ish file/signal operation in `lib/run.sh` so it is unit-testable and also drivable non-interactively (`almanac hub --watch|--stop|--steer <id>`, the seam the gum menu and scripts/tests share). `almanac_loop_run_signal_file <type> <kind>` maps a run type to the dot-file its runner watches between rounds (`.ralph-stop`/`.ralph-steer`, `.harden-stop`/`.harden-steer`) — it dispatches to the loop adapter's `signal_file` control verb (see the loop-adapter seam below) rather than branching on type, so each loop owns its own signal files. `almanac_loop_run_stop` writes that stop file under the run's root (its working dir) **and** best-effort `TERM`s a live numeric pid (never a dead/finished one), so a run tears down at its next between-round check or immediately if blocked mid-round. `almanac_loop_run_steer` writes the directive to the steer file the next round consumes (ralph reads `.ralph-steer`), rejecting a blank directive. `almanac_loop_run_detail` is the pure per-run detail render (identity, live status — upgraded to `stale` when a running pid is gone — round/summary, start/finish); `almanac_loop_run_watch` renders it once off a TTY (the scripts-safe path) and redraws it on a `follow` TTY until the run reaches a terminal status. On a TTY, `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_menu` opens a selection menu whose top entry is always **+ New run**, followed by each running loop (each row carries `[id]`) and `quit`; selecting a running loop opens a watch/stop/steer/back action menu over it. The menu opens even with an empty registry (so New run is reachable with no prior runs), and **degrades gracefully without gum**: with gum it is a styled `gum choose`/`gum input`/`gum confirm`, and without gum it renders as a plain numbered menu read over stdin — both built on the shared `almanac_loop_ui_choose`/`almanac_loop_ui_input`/`almanac_loop_ui_confirm` seam, so the same menus work on any TTY. Off a TTY (piped/captured) the hub still renders the read-only overview once and exits. The **new-run** flow is itself built on a pure composer + non-interactive seam, mirroring the per-run actions: `almanac_loop_new_run_argv <type> [key=value…]` composes the launcher argv (one token per line, leading with the `ralph`/`harden` subcommand) — ralph config maps to ralph.sh flags (`--prd`/`--mode`/`--provider`/`--model`/`--effort`/`--iterations`/`--no-oversee`), harden launches the convergence loop (`harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`); `almanac_loop_new_run_env <type> [key=value…]` composes the `KEY=VALUE` env a run needs beyond its argv (ralph emits none — all flags; harden's reviewer config rides on `HARDEN_LENSES`/`HARDEN_PROVIDER`/`HARDEN_MODEL`/`HARDEN_EFFORT`). Both return 1 for an unknown type and 2 for a missing required field (ralph: prd; harden: target), so a malformed run is rejected rather than launched. `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_launch_new` either prints the resolved `ENV… almanac <argv>` command (dry-run, the scripts-safe preview the gum confirm reuses) or exports the env and execs the launcher, handing over the terminal. The interactive New-run flow (`almanac_hub_new_run`) picks the loop type, then hands off to the shared **almanac launcher** (`almanac_loop_launch`, see the CLI section) — no run-config logic lives in the hub any more; the same launcher backs `almanac ralph` / `almanac harden`, so the New-run UX is identical to running those directly. (The non-interactive `--new` composer seam below stays for scripts/tests.) The seam is also drivable directly: `almanac hub --new <ralph|harden> [config…] [--dry-run]`. `tests/test-hub.sh` pins the non-interactive seams (`--steer` writes the steer file with the directive, `--stop` writes the stop file, `--watch` renders the run detail once off a TTY, `--new … --dry-run` composes the ralph/harden launch and rejects a run missing its required config). The argv/env composers themselves do **not** branch on loop type — each loop adapter implements `new_run_argv` / `new_run_env` as additional contract verbs (alongside `launch` / `launch_usage` / `exec_argv`), and `almanac_loop_new_run_argv` / `_env` in `lib/run.sh` are thin dispatchers that validate the loop name (`almanac_loop_adapter_known` → 1 for unknown) and forward the key=value stream through `almanac_loop_adapter_call`. Adding a 4th loop adds the two composer verbs to its adapter; the dispatcher does not change.\n80\t\n81\tThe `harden-loop` skill (`skills/loop/harden-loop/SKILL.md`) is the agent-facing companion to the harden CLI - it teaches the convergence contract, the round structure, lenses, per-role provider config, supervision, and gum-optional degradation, pointing at `almanac harden <target>`. The `converge-loop` skill (`skills/loop/converge-loop/SKILL.md`) is the companion to `almanac converge` - it teaches the generic goal/exec loop, mutable goal state, overseer contract, role config, supervision, and when to choose converge instead of ralph or harden. Like `ralph-loop`, both are `disable-model-invocation: true` heavyweight autonomous loops: user-invocable via `/almanac:harden-loop` or `/almanac:converge-loop`, and loadable by orchestrators without bloating the auto-listing.\n82\t\n83\t## Shared Loop Engine (`lib/run.sh`, `lib/worker.sh` + focused modules)\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b01beec-8e7f-499f-84c4-979f364c4a47","uuid":"1d527229-c0c2-4a4e-bc04-06d815da7bf0","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:28:58.882Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md","content":"| `skills/loop/harden-loop/` | User-facing harden-loop skill. |\n| `skills/loop/converge-loop/` | User-facing converge-loop skill. |\n\n**The almanac launcher (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`).** Interactive run *configuration* is unified in one loop-agnostic launcher, `almanac_loop_launch <loop> [native flags…]`. The launcher itself does **not** branch on loop type — it dispatches the adapter's `launch` verb through the loop-adapter seam, so each loop's flag parsing, prompts, env-export, and runner exec live in its adapter file (`lib/loops/<name>.sh::almanac_loop_<name>_launch`). Adding a 4th loop is a single new file in `lib/loops/`; no central case-statement to keep in sync. What the launcher does still own is what every adapter uses identically: the field collectors (`_almanac_launch_need_provider`/`_choice`/`_positive_int` — pick a provider, resolve a model/effort, validate-and-re-prompt for numeric fields so a stray keystroke is a retry, not a crash) and the summary panel (`almanac_loop_launch_summary` — gum-styled or plain), all routed through the shared `almanac_loop_ui_*` gum-or-plain seam. Each adapter's `launch` verb summarises, confirms, and execs the runner — it never reimplements the run, only its config. The runner exec routes through the same adapter's `exec_argv` verb (`almanac_loop_adapter_call <type> exec_argv …` → `_ALMANAC_LOOP_ARGV`, see the loop-adapter seam below), so no central code hard-codes a loop's runner path: ralph's `…/ralph-loop/scripts/once.sh`/`afk.sh`, harden's `almanac harden <target> --loop`, and converge's `almanac converge --goal …` all live in their adapters. `cmd/ralph.sh`, the ralph skill `ralph.sh` (a thin delegate, so direct `bash ralph.sh` and `almanac ralph` share it), and the hub's New-run flow all route through `almanac_loop_launch`, so the config UX is identical everywhere and has a single source of truth.\n\n`cmd/doctor.sh` reports almanac's **optional-dependency** status so users know what is and isn't styled/available: `gum` (Charm — styles the harden/ralph dashboards + HITL prompts), `gh` (GitHub issue queues + CI watch), and `jq` (findings parsing). The gum check is the binary-presence helper `almanac_gum_present` (in `lib/core.sh`), distinct from the runtime, TTY-gated `almanac_loop_ui_has_gum` — `doctor` and the installers want \"is the binary installed\", not \"should I style this stream right now\". `almanac_report_gum` prints the verdict with an install hint, and both the claude-code and codex installers call it at the end of `almanac install`, so install **and** doctor surface gum's presence/absence. Every optional dep is exactly that — absent, the CLI degrades to plain output, keeping almanac's near-zero-dependency promise.\n\n`cmd/harden.sh` is the first harden-loop CLI surface. It uses `lib/harden-core.sh` to draft a target-specific `rubric.md` under `docs/plans/harden/<target-slug>/` in the caller repo, refusing to overwrite an existing contract. Drafts start with `Status: draft`; after the developer edits the contract, `almanac harden <target> --approve` marks it `Status: approved` and appends an approval timestamp. The approval is a **lock the loop enforces**: once a rubric has been drafted, `almanac_harden_rubric_approved` gates the fan-out, so a bare `almanac harden <target>` refuses (via `_die`) to run reviewers against an unapproved contract — the goalposts can only be set by a deliberate human approve. An explicit ad-hoc run with no rubric at all bypasses the gate and proceeds. The rubric is the **bar**: `almanac_harden_rubric_acceptance` reads the `## Acceptance` criteria back, and `almanac_harden_reviewer_prompt` embeds them so reviewers judge against the contract rather than an implicit standard. The bar grows only by ratified defects — `almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion` appends a new `- [ ]` criterion under `## Acceptance` append-only (monotonic, visible as a diff) and idempotent (an identical criterion is never re-added). Run with no flags, `almanac harden <target>` fans out one read-only reviewer per configured lens (`almanac_harden_fanout`), passing each reviewer the rubric path so the contract is consumed when present. The lens set comes from `almanac_harden_lenses` — the five PRD lenses (`correctness security perf edge-cases contracts`) by default, overridable at runtime via `HARDEN_LENSES` (comma- or whitespace-separated, no enforced cap). Each lens spawns a background worker through the shared worker orchestration with a `read-only` sandbox, resolving its provider/model/effort via `almanac_harden_role` (see **Per-role provider config** below) so lenses can mix providers. Reviewers run concurrently and never write to the target; once all finish, the parent aggregates each reviewer's findings into the ledger **sequentially** (so there are no concurrent ledger writers), deduped via `almanac_harden_ledger_record`, and prints each lens's findings (jq parse with an awk fallback). Worker pids are captured via a file rather than command substitution so the backgrounded workers stay children of the launching shell and remain waitable; a failed reviewer is reported and skipped without sinking the round. A missing target fails via `_die` before anything spawns.\n\nThe rubric is **immutable to agents during a run** — the goalposts move only by a deliberate human act (editing/approving the file between runs) or the conductor's controlled append at ratification (`almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion`, which runs outside the reviewer/fixer phase). The fan-out brackets the reviewer round with an integrity guard: `almanac_harden_rubric_snapshot` copies the rubric before any reviewer spawns and `almanac_harden_rubric_verify` checks it byte-for-byte once they finish, reverting and warning if it changed. Reviewers run read-only and cannot write the rubric, so this is defense-in-depth today; it becomes load-bearing for the write-capable fixer slice, which brackets its own agent call the same way (snapshot after ratification's legitimate appends, revert any fixer edit). An ad-hoc bare run with no drafted rubric has nothing to guard, so the snapshot returns cleanly and the guard is skipped.\n\nThe **findings ledger** persists adjudicated findings append-only to `findings.md` beside the rubric. The canonical finding schema is `lens, severity, location, claim, demonstration`; the ledger adds `id`, `status` (`open` | `fixed` | `rejected-subjective` | `wontfix-per-context`), `round`, and an `adjudication` note. `almanac_harden_parse_findings` normalizes a reviewer's JSON-Lines result into ledger-entry rows (new findings are `open`), dropping malformed lines cleanly. `id` is a `cksum` fingerprint of `(lens, location, claim)` — demonstration is excluded so a reworded repro is not a new finding — and `almanac_harden_ledger_record` dedupes against it, so a finding already adjudicated in a prior round is never re-added. `almanac_harden_ledger_open_blocking` reads the ledger back and returns only `status: open` findings, since fixed and rejected findings never gate the loop.\n\nThe **ratification engine** decides blocking vs. note by *executing* a finding's demonstration, never by trusting a reviewer's assertion. `almanac_harden_ratify` runs the demonstration through the execution seam `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces` — the **real ratify-by-execution executor** (PRD story 8): it builds a conductor prompt (`almanac_harden_ratify_prompt`) that tells the conductor to carry out the demonstration against the current code and end with a single machine token, runs it through the resolved conductor `(provider, model, effort)` via the shared agent runner (`workspace-write`, like the fixer, since judging by execution means actually running the proposed test/input), and reads the verdict back with `almanac_harden_ratify_verdict` (the affirmative literal `HARDEN_VERDICT=reproduces`; the negative cannot match it). It is conservative on every uncertainty — an empty demonstration, an unresolved provider, a crashed/un-runnable conductor call, or an un-parseable verdict all read as non-reproducing (a note) so opinions never silently gate the loop and a flaky provider never blocks a run. Tests drive the decision paths behind a fake provider (no real model calls). `almanac_harden_ratify` records the outcome in the ledger, printing one verdict: a new/open finding that reproduces → `blocking` (`status: open`); one that does not → `note` (`status: rejected-subjective`); an already-adjudicated finding (`fixed` / `rejected-subjective` / `wontfix-per-context`) that *newly* reproduces → `reopened` (back to `status: open`); one that still does not reproduce → `dropped` (left untouched, never re-litigated). Status transitions go through `almanac_harden_ledger_set_status` (rewrites status + adjudication in place); `almanac_harden_ledger_status` reads a finding's current status so new findings are told from already-adjudicated ones. When `almanac_harden_ratify` is given a rubric path, a finding that reproduces (`blocking` or `reopened`) also appends a falsifiable criterion to the rubric's `## Acceptance` via `almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion`, so the bar grows monotonically with every ratified defect; notes and drops never touch the rubric, so opinions cannot raise it.\n\nThe **single sequential fixer** (`almanac harden <target> --fix` → `almanac_harden_fix`) applies the open blocking findings. v1 is deliberately one write-capable agent — `sandbox=workspace-write` (never read-only), run in place with **no worktree** — so there are never concurrent writers to reconcile and any regression test it generates simply persists in the working tree (PRD: \"the deliverable is hardened code *plus the regression suite the loop built to prove it*\"). It reads the kill-list from `almanac_harden_ledger_open_blocking`, renders it into `almanac_harden_fixer_prompt` (which also embeds the rubric bar and tells the fixer to leave a failing-then-passing regression test per finding), and runs the fixer through the shared agent runner. The fixer's agent call is bracketed by the same rubric immutability guard the fan-out uses (`almanac_harden_rubric_snapshot` before, `almanac_harden_rubric_verify` after) — load-bearing here because the fixer *can* write the rubric; legitimate rubric growth happens earlier in ratification, outside this bracket. On a clean fixer exit every open blocking finding is marked `fixed` in the ledger (a still-broken one is reopened next round by reviewers/ratify, so nothing is silently closed); a fixer failure leaves the findings open and returns the provider's exit code. With no open blocking findings the fixer is a no-op (spawns no agent). The objective feedback gate is **decoupled** from the fixer so it can run once per round even when there is nothing to fix: `almanac_harden_report_feedback` runs the shared runner and prints a pass/fail verdict per loop. The convergence loop calls it as the round's last step (and reads its aggregate as the round's exit code); the standalone `--fix` CLI path runs it right after the fixer so its output is unchanged.\n\nThe **convergence loop** (`almanac harden <target> --loop` → `almanac_harden_run`) repeats hardening rounds until the target is bulletproof or the round budget is hit. One round (`almanac_harden_round`) runs **fan-out → ratify → fix → feedback** in sequence, threading the round number; its exit code is the feedback verdict (0 = all loops green). `almanac_harden_ratify_open` adjudicates every open finding the fan-out recorded by executing its demonstration through the seam (reproducing → stays blocking and grows the rubric bar; otherwise → non-blocking note); that rubric append is the conductor's sanctioned growth and runs *between* fan-out and fix, outside the immutability brackets those phases use. After each round `almanac_harden_run` prints the **kill-list** (the blocking findings still open) and a **verdict**. The verdict comes from the **convergence gate** `almanac_harden_gate_verdict` — a pure predicate over `(acceptance_met, open_blocking, round, budget)` with no I/O, unit-tested across its whole input space: it returns `converged` (exit 0) when acceptance is met **and** zero open blocking findings remain, `non-converged` (exit 2) when the budget is reached without convergence, and `continue` (exit 1) otherwise. Convergence is checked first, so a round that converges exactly at the budget is never mislabelled, and the gate never returns `continue` once the budget is reached, so the loop always terminates. Acceptance for the gate combines the round's feedback verdict with `almanac_harden_acceptance_met` (no unchecked `- [ ]` criteria left in the rubric's `## Acceptance`; vacuously met for an ad-hoc run with no rubric). The budget is configurable via `--rounds N` (default 5, or `HARDEN_ROUND_BUDGET`). On a `continue` verdict the loop pauses at a **HITL checkpoint** (`almanac_harden_hitl_checkpoint`) offering three choices: `HARDEN_HITL` (`ship`|`continue`|`steer`) drives it non-interactively, a TTY prompts via `gum choose`/`gum input` when present and a plain `read` otherwise, and an unattended run defaults to continue. **Ship** stops the loop and accepts the current state; **continue** runs another round; **steer** captures a directive (from `HARDEN_STEER` or the prompt, echoed on the checkpoint's stdout with the return code `2`) that `almanac_harden_run` carries forward and threads — via `almanac_harden_round` → `almanac_harden_fanout`/`almanac_harden_fix` → the reviewer and fixer prompts — into every subsequent round, so the operator can redirect or amend the run between rounds without babysitting each worker (PRD story 18). The directive persists until steered again; a rubric amendment is also picked up between rounds, since the fan-out re-reads and re-snapshots `rubric.md` each round.\n\n`cmd/converge.sh` is the generic loop sibling to ralph and harden. It launches with `almanac converge --goal <goal> --exec <cmd> [--rounds N] [--no-oversee] [--oversee-every N]`, stores canonical state under `docs/plans/converge/<slug>/`, registers `type=converge` in the shared run registry, and can later render status, watch, or stop via `almanac converge <slug> [--watch|--stop]`. `lib/converge-core.sh` owns the whole converge lifecycle: slug/scaffold, worker prompt and editable `prompt.md`, read-only overseer prompt, conservative four-field verdict parse, one-shot `.converge-steer`, `.converge-stop`, direct `goal.md` mutation with `goal.history.log`, final `convergence.md`, dashboard frame, and EXIT-trap-safe run finalization. Unlike ralph's PRD queue or harden's lens fan-out, converge runs exactly one user-supplied command per round; convergence is an overseer judgment against the current mutable goal. The worker and overseer both use `lib/agent.sh`, `lib/role.sh`, and `lib/run.sh` directly, so converge is a first-class shared-engine consumer rather than a fork.\n\n**Per-role provider config.** Every harden role selects its own `(provider, model, effort)` through `almanac_harden_role <role> [lens]` (with `almanac_harden_role_field` returning a single field), the one place harden's per-role defaults live. The three roles are **conductor** (judges/ratifies), **reviewer** (one per lens), and **fixer** (the single sequential writer). It layers over the shared loop resolver, most specific first: `HARDEN_<ROLE>[_<LENS>]_{PROVIDER,MODEL,EFFORT}` → `HARDEN_<ROLE>_*` → `HARDEN_*` → harden's default (provider `claude`; the provider's own default model/effort). Only reviewers consult the lens, so providers can be **mixed across lenses** in one round; conductor and fixer ignore it. Resolution reads only `HARDEN_*` env — never any host marker — so the tuple is identical whether the run was launched from Claude Code or Codex. The fan-out resolves each reviewer per lens and the fixer resolves its own; the convergence loop announces the resolved conductor at start and hands its identity to the execution seam (`almanac_harden_demo_reproduces`, via `almanac_harden_ratify`) for every ratified finding, so the provider that *judges* findings — by actually executing each demonstration through the agent runner — is the configured conductor, mixable per role and independent of the launching host.\n\nConverge roles use the same resolver with a `CONVERGE_` prefix: `CONVERGE_AGENT_{PROVIDER,MODEL,EFFORT}` controls the write-capable worker, `CONVERGE_OVERSEER_{PROVIDER,MODEL,EFFORT}` controls the read-only overseer, and `CONVERGE_{PROVIDER,MODEL,EFFORT}` is the consumer-wide fallback. Adding another loop consumer should mirror this pattern: define role names, document the env prefix, route calls through `almanac_loop_role_field`/`almanac_loop_role_config`, and keep provider defaults out of command parsing.\n\n**Supervision dashboard.** A run is supervised through a `gum`-styled redraw dashboard composed from live run state: reviewer status (per lens, with health), the round, findings tallies, rubric progress, and the feedback verdict. Following the PRD's \"render logic is pure\" rule, the composer `almanac_harden_dashboard_rows` is a **pure function** — it reads worker rows from stdin as `id<TAB>provider<TAB>health` TSV and takes the round/budget, findings tally, rubric progress, and feedback verdict as args, returning the printable rows with no file I/O, clock, or terminal, so it is unit-tested across all five fields without a terminal. The gather helpers feed it real state: `almanac_harden_dashboard_worker_rows` reads each worker's `status.tsv` and classifies its health via the shared detector; `almanac_harden_findings_tally` counts the ledger by status (`open=N fixed=N notes=N`, where notes = `rejected-subjective` + `wontfix-per-context`); `almanac_harden_rubric_progress` reports `checked/total` over the `## Acceptance` checklist; and `almanac_harden_latest_run_id` locates the target's most recent run by directory mtime (a glob+mtime scan, not `ls -t | head`, which would trip SIGPIPE under `set -o pipefail`). `almanac_harden_render_dashboard` ties these together and wraps the composed rows in `almanac_loop_ui_render`, which styles them as a rounded `gum` panel on a TTY and **degrades to plain passthrough** when `gum` is absent (or `ALMANAC_NO_GUM=1`, or output is piped) — keeping almanac's near-zero-dependency promise. It is safe to call before any run/worker/rubric state exists: it degrades to an empty reviewer list and zeroed tallies rather than failing.\n\nThe dashboard is **watched live** through `almanac_harden_dashboard_redraw` (the `almanac harden <target> --watch` mode and the hub's \"watch a run\" action): a redraw loop that, each frame, clears the terminal (TTY only, via the shared `almanac_loop_ui_clear` — never polluting piped output) and reprints `almanac_harden_render_dashboard` from the run's live worker/ledger/rubric state, so reviewer health, findings tallies, rubric progress, and the verdict update as the run progresses. Termination is bounded by construction so it can never run forever: a positive frame cap renders exactly that many frames (the unit-test path); off a TTY an uncapped call renders a single frame and returns (so a pipe/script never blocks); on a TTY it redraws until the latest run's workers have all finished (`almanac_harden_run_has_active_worker`, which keys on each round's per-worker `status.tsv` for liveness — the loop-level run-status blob tracks round/verdict) or there is no run to watch. A single worker's live event stream is watched through `almanac_harden_watch_worker` (`--watch-worker <lens|worker-id>`), which resolves the target's most recent run, accepts a bare lens as shorthand for its `reviewer-<lens>` worker, and delegates to the shared `almanac_loop_worker_watch` — tailing the worker's `events.jsonl` live (`tail -f`) on a TTY and printing its current contents once otherwise. The **steer flow** completes the supervision half: the HITL checkpoint's `steer` choice (above) lets the operator redirect or amend a run between rounds, threading a directive into the subsequent rounds' agents.\n\n**Interactive hub.** Bare `almanac` in a TTY (and `almanac hub` always) opens the **hub** (`cmd/hub.sh`) — the front door to every loop, built as a thin reader over the run registry rather than a retrofit. Its read views are **pure registry projections** so they are testable off a terminal and degrade to plain text without gum. `almanac_loop_hub_render <root> <which> [limit]` projects the registry into one of two lists: `running` emits every run whose lifecycle status is still `running`, carrying its live `round`/`summary` from the run-status blob, and surfaces a running entry whose pid is gone as `stale` (via `almanac_loop_run_is_stale`) so a crashed run is visible rather than silently lost; `recent` emits the finished runs (done/failed/aborted) newest-finish-first, capped at `limit` (default 10). `almanac_loop_hub_overview` composes both sections (with empty-state lines) and frames them through the shared `almanac_loop_ui_render`, so the hub shows a rounded gum panel on a TTY and plain text without gum. Off a TTY `cmd/hub.sh` renders that overview once and exits — it never blocks on a menu, so `almanac hub | cat`, scripts, and tests get a clean read view (`tests/test-hub.sh` drives the real `bin/almanac` chain: bare-almanac-off-a-TTY prints help and does *not* open the hub; `almanac hub` renders the running + recent overview from a seeded registry). The **per-run actions** layer on this read view: selecting a running loop offers **watch**, **stop**, and **queue-steer**, each a pure-ish file/signal operation in `lib/run.sh` so it is unit-testable and also drivable non-interactively (`almanac hub --watch|--stop|--steer <id>`, the seam the gum menu and scripts/tests share). `almanac_loop_run_signal_file <type> <kind>` maps a run type to the dot-file its runner watches between rounds (`.ralph-stop`/`.ralph-steer`, `.harden-stop`/`.harden-steer`) — it dispatches to the loop adapter's `signal_file` control verb (see the loop-adapter seam below) rather than branching on type, so each loop owns its own signal files. `almanac_loop_run_stop` writes that stop file under the run's root (its working dir) **and** best-effort `TERM`s a live numeric pid (never a dead/finished one), so a run tears down at its next between-round check or immediately if blocked mid-round. `almanac_loop_run_steer` writes the directive to the steer file the next round consumes (ralph reads `.ralph-steer`), rejecting a blank directive. `almanac_loop_run_detail` is the pure per-run detail render (identity, live status — upgraded to `stale` when a running pid is gone — round/summary, start/finish); `almanac_loop_run_watch` renders it once off a TTY (the scripts-safe path) and redraws it on a `follow` TTY until the run reaches a terminal status. On a TTY, `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_menu` opens a selection menu whose top entry is always **+ New run**, followed by each running loop (each row carries `[id]`) and `quit`; selecting a running loop opens a watch/stop/steer/back action menu over it. The menu opens even with an empty registry (so New run is reachable with no prior runs), and **degrades gracefully without gum**: with gum it is a styled `gum choose`/`gum input`/`gum confirm`, and without gum it renders as a plain numbered menu read over stdin — both built on the shared `almanac_loop_ui_choose`/`almanac_loop_ui_input`/`almanac_loop_ui_confirm` seam, so the same menus work on any TTY. Off a TTY (piped/captured) the hub still renders the read-only overview once and exits. The **new-run** flow is itself built on a pure composer + non-interactive seam, mirroring the per-run actions: `almanac_loop_new_run_argv <type> [key=value…]` composes the launcher argv (one token per line, leading with the `ralph`/`harden` subcommand) — ralph config maps to ralph.sh flags (`--prd`/`--mode`/`--provider`/`--model`/`--effort`/`--iterations`/`--no-oversee`), harden launches the convergence loop (`harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`); `almanac_loop_new_run_env <type> [key=value…]` composes the `KEY=VALUE` env a run needs beyond its argv (ralph emits none — all flags; harden's reviewer config rides on `HARDEN_LENSES`/`HARDEN_PROVIDER`/`HARDEN_MODEL`/`HARDEN_EFFORT`). Both return 1 for an unknown type and 2 for a missing required field (ralph: prd; harden: target), so a malformed run is rejected rather than launched. `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_launch_new` either prints the resolved `ENV… almanac <argv>` command (dry-run, the scripts-safe preview the gum confirm reuses) or exports the env and execs the launcher, handing over the terminal. The interactive New-run flow (`almanac_hub_new_run`) picks the loop type, then hands off to the shared **almanac launcher** (`almanac_loop_launch`, see the CLI section) — no run-config logic lives in the hub any more; the same launcher backs `almanac ralph` / `almanac harden`, so the New-run UX is identical to running those directly. (The non-interactive `--new` composer seam below stays for scripts/tests.) The seam is also drivable directly: `almanac hub --new <ralph|harden> [config…] [--dry-run]`. `tests/test-hub.sh` pins the non-interactive seams (`--steer` writes the steer file with the directive, `--stop` writes the stop file, `--watch` renders the run detail once off a TTY, `--new … --dry-run` composes the ralph/harden launch and rejects a run missing its required config). The argv/env composers themselves do **not** branch on loop type — each loop adapter implements `new_run_argv` / `new_run_env` as additional contract verbs (alongside `launch` / `launch_usage` / `exec_argv`), and `almanac_loop_new_run_argv` / `_env` in `lib/run.sh` are thin dispatchers that validate the loop name (`almanac_loop_adapter_known` → 1 for unknown) and forward the key=value stream through `almanac_loop_adapter_call`. Adding a 4th loop adds the two composer verbs to its adapter; the dispatcher does not change.\n\nThe `harden-loop` skill (`skills/loop/harden-loop/SKILL.md`) is the agent-facing companion to the harden CLI - it teaches the convergence contract, the round structure, lenses, per-role provider config, supervision, and gum-optional degradation, pointing at `almanac harden <target>`. The `converge-loop` skill (`skills/loop/converge-loop/SKILL.md`) is the companion to `almanac converge` - it teaches the generic goal/exec loop, mutable goal state, overseer contract, role config, supervision, and when to choose converge instead of ralph or harden. Like `ralph-loop`, both are `disable-model-invocation: true` heavyweight autonomous loops: user-invocable via `/almanac:harden-loop` or `/almanac:converge-loop`, and loadable by orchestrators without bloating the auto-listing.\n\n## Shared Loop Engine (`lib/run.sh`, `lib/worker.sh` + focused modules)\n","numLines":35,"startLine":50,"totalLines":111}}}
